There are many types of reverse shells, including those that use TCP, UDP, and HTTP. This guide will cover how to make a reverse shell that uses TCP.
The reverse shell will be a 32-bit Windows executable. It will connect back to the attacker's machine on port 8080.
The attacker's machine will need to be running a listener program. A listener program is a program that waits for a connection on a port. When a connection is made, the listener program spawns a new shell.
There are many listener programs available. This guide will cover how to use the Netcat listener program.
Netcat is a listener program that can be used to create a TCP listener on any port. It is available for both Windows and Unix-based operating systems.
Here is how to use Netcat to create a listener on port 8080:
